@startuml sequence-report-incident
' What happens, class by class, when an employee presses "Report incident".
title I-ITSM · Sequence: an employee reports an incident

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam ParticipantPadding 8
autonumber

actor Employee
participant "Spring Security\nfilters" as Security
participant "EmployeeController" as Controller
participant "IncidentService" as Service
participant "IncidentFactory" as Factory
participant "Incident" as Incident
participant "IncidentEvent\nPublisher" as Publisher
participant "DuplicateDetector" as Duplicates
participant "PriorityCalculator\n(Weighted…)" as Priority
participant "SlaService" as Sla
participant "AssignmentService" as Assignment
participant "AssignmentStrategy\n(SkillBased…)" as Strategy
participant "AuditTrailListener" as Audit
participant "NotificationListener" as Notifier
participant "NotificationDispatcher" as Dispatcher
participant "LiveUpdateListener\n+ LiveUpdates" as Live
database "H2" as DB
actor "Open pages of\nadmins, technicians" as Others

Employee -> Security : POST /employee/incidents\n(title, description, service, category, impact, urgency)
Security -> Security : session valid? role EMPLOYEE? CSRF token?
Security -> Controller : report(user, form)
Controller -> Controller : Bean Validation on IncidentForm

group one database transaction
  Controller -> Service : report(reporter, title, …)
  Service -> DB : find service; same report already open?
  note right of Service
    ServiceNotFoundException or
    DuplicateIncidentException end it here.
    No category chosen: CategorySuggester picks one.
  end note

  Service -> Factory : create(reporter, title, …)
  Factory -> Incident ** : new Incident(…)  status NEW
  Factory -> DB : save  (code INC-0007 from the sequence)
  Factory --> Service : incident

  Service -> Publisher : publish(REPORTED)
  Publisher -> Audit : onIncidentEvent
  Audit -> DB : insert audit entry
  Publisher -> Notifier : onIncidentEvent
  Notifier -> Dispatcher : sendAfterCommit(messages)
  Publisher -> Live : onIncidentEvent (remembered until the commit)

  Service -> Duplicates : findDuplicate(incident, recent open ones)
  Duplicates --> Service : match or nothing
  Service -> Priority : calculate(incident)
  Priority --> Service : PriorityAssessment(P1, score 27)
  Service -> Sla : policyFor(P1)
  Sla --> Service : SlaPolicy(5 min, 30 min)
  Service -> Incident : prioritise(assessment, policy)
  Incident -> Incident : NewState.onPrioritised():\ntransitionTo(CLASSIFIED)
  Service -> Publisher : publish(PRIORITISED)

  Service -> Assignment : autoAssign(incident)
  Assignment -> DB : technicians with their open-incident counts
  Assignment -> Strategy : choose(incident, loads)
  Strategy --> Assignment : technician
  Assignment -> Incident : assignTo(technician)
  Incident -> Incident : ClassifiedState.onAssigned():\ntransitionTo(ASSIGNED)
  Assignment -> Publisher : publish(ASSIGNED)
  note right of Assignment
    Nobody can take it: the incident stays
    CLASSIFIED, ASSIGNMENT_FAILED is published
    and the administrators are told.
  end note

  Service -> Sla : refresh(incident, now)
  Service --> Controller : incident
end

Controller --> Employee : 302 redirect to /employee/incidents/INC-0007

group after the commit, each channel in its own transaction
  Dispatcher -> DB : in-app notifications for reporter and technician
  Dispatcher -> Live : unread count of each recipient
  Live --> Others : event "bell": the number on the bell changes
  Dispatcher -> DB : simulated emails into the outbox
  Live --> Others : event "incident": dashboards and queues\nfetch their content again (one message, not three)
  note right of Dispatcher
    A channel that fails is logged and skipped.
    The incident and the other channels are unaffected.
  end note
end

Employee -> Controller : GET /employee/incidents/INC-0007
Controller --> Employee : incident page: P1 · Assigned, deadlines, timeline
@enduml
